---
import CodeBlock from '../CodeBlock.astro';
import StepCard from '../StepCard.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s3">
  <div class="num">03</div>
  <h2>Install Claude Code</h2>
  <p class="lede">You'll use the terminal for a few setup steps.</p>
  <p>
    A terminal is a window for typing commands. On a Mac, open Terminal using
    Spotlight. On Windows, open PowerShell from Start. The steps here cover
    those two systems; Linux users should follow the linked installation page.
    Press Enter after typing or pasting each terminal command. If you have never
    used a terminal, Anthropic's
    <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/terminal-guide"
      >terminal guide for new users</a
    > shows how to open one and paste a command.
  </p>
  <p>
    Claude Code requires internet access and a paid Claude plan or supported API
    access; the free Claude plan does not include it. Check the current account
    requirements on
    <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/setup#authenticate"
      >Claude Code's setup page</a
    > before paying. For this exercise, I would use an eligible subscription rather
    than set up separately billed API access.
  </p>
  <p>
    Claude Code also has a
    <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/desktop-quickstart">desktop app</a>
    that needs no terminal. This guide uses the terminal version, so its commands
    and prompts match what you will see. If you choose the app instead, select Plan
    mode before sending a request and follow the same review steps.
  </p>
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="1" title={'Install and check it'}>
      <p>
        On the setup page, check that your computer meets the requirements. Copy
        the install command for macOS or Windows PowerShell into your terminal
        and press Enter. This downloads and runs Anthropic's installer, so take
        it from that official page, not an AI reply. On Windows, the PowerShell
        command only works when the line you type on starts with
        <code class="i">PS</code>; the page has a separate command for the older
        Command Prompt.
      </p>
      <p>
        The installer shows no progress while it downloads, so give it a minute.
        When it finishes, open a new terminal window and run the check below. A
        version number means the installation worked. If it says the command was
        not found, stop here and use Anthropic's
        <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/troubleshoot-install"
          >installation troubleshooting page</a
        >.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Terminal: check installation'}
    code={'claude --version'}
    copy
  />
  <div class="steps">
    <StepCard number="2" title={'Make and enter your folder'}>
      <p>
        In Finder on a Mac or File Explorer on Windows, make a new folder called
        <code class="i">my-site</code>. In the terminal, type <code class="i"
          >cd</code
        >
        followed by a space. On a Mac, drag the folder into the window. On Windows,
        copy its full location from File Explorer's address bar and paste it between
        straight double quotes. Press Enter. This changes which folder the terminal
        is working in.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
    <StepCard number="3" title={'Start and sign in'}>
      <p>
        Run the command below to start in Plan mode, so you can review the
        proposed changes before they are made. Follow the browser sign-in
        prompts with your eligible Claude account. If asked to trust a folder,
        check it is your new <code class="i">my-site</code> folder.
      </p>
    </StepCard>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Terminal: start in Plan mode'}
    code={'claude --permission-mode plan'}
    copy
  />
  <p>
    Always start with this full command. If you type only
    <code class="i">claude</code>, current versions usually begin in Auto mode,
    which can change files without asking you first.
  </p>
  <p>
    Check that the status line at the bottom of the terminal reads
    <code class="i">plan mode on</code>. Once you send a request, Claude will
    propose a plan. If it matches your request, choose "Yes, and use auto mode"
    if available, or "Yes, manually approve edits". If it does not, choose "No,
    keep planning" and explain what needs changing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Approval leaves Plan mode. Before requesting another change, start your
    message with <code class="i">/plan</code>. Never enable bypass permissions.
  </p>
  <details class="card">
    <summary>About these safety controls</summary>
    <div
      style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px; padding-top: 16px"
    >
      <p>
        Plan mode can read files and run exploration commands, so it is not a
        sandbox or a promise that nothing runs. It blocks source edits until you
        approve the plan, except in interactive terminal sessions where bypass
        permissions are available in the mode cycle, even if not selected. Do
        not use flags that make bypass permissions available.
      </p>
      <p>
        Auto mode reduces routine permission prompts with background safety
        checks. Routine file edits inside the working folder may happen without
        a separate AI safety check. It is not bypass permissions, a sandbox or a
        guarantee of safety. You still check the finished page yourself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Auto availability depends on your version, selected model, provider,
        organization settings and service availability. If Auto is unavailable,
        choose manual approval, not "auto-accept edits", which is a different
        mode. To start directly in Manual mode, use
        <code class="i">claude --permission-mode default</code>, then prefix
        each change request with <code class="i">/plan</code>. Manual normally
        asks before edits and commands, subject to permission rules; labels vary
        by version. Read each proposed action and use the displayed keys to
        allow or deny it. If unclear, ask the person helping you. See the
        current <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permission-modes"
          >permission docs</a
        >.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shift+Tab switches between modes, and the status line at the bottom of
        the terminal names the current one. If it shows a mode you did not
        choose, press Shift+Tab until it reads <code class="i"
          >plan mode on</code
        >. Do not press Shift+Tab while Claude is asking you to approve a plan:
        there, it approves the plan. Press Escape first.
      </p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <h3>Give it standing instructions</h3>
  <p>
    <code class="i">CLAUDE.md</code> is a plain text file of project instructions
    Claude Code reads at the start of a session, so you do not have to repeat your
    preferences every time. <strong
      >It is guidance, not enforced protection.</strong
    > The rule about photos below does not lock the folder or prevent Claude from
    modifying it. That is why you use copies and keep backups outside the project.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Claude Code, type "/plan Create CLAUDE.md with exactly the following
    text, and change nothing else", then paste this block into the same message.
    Review the plan and choose Auto or manual approval as described above.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    label={'Example CLAUDE.md'}
    code={'# About this project\n\nThis is my one-page personal website.\n\n# Rules\n\nShow me a short plan and wait for my ok before changing files.\nExplain things briefly in plain English. I am not a programmer.\nDo not add anything I did not ask for.\nDo not modify the photos folder or work outside this project.\nDo not upload or publish files, install software or change permission settings without asking me.'}
    copy
  />
  <p>
    Ask it to show the saved file so you can check it. The request to wait in
    this file is still only an instruction; it does not turn Plan mode on. Keep
    using <code class="i">/plan</code> before each new change.
  </p>
  <p>
    Setup complete? Go straight to <a href="#s5">Build your website</a> and use the
    supplied request. The <a href="#s4">prompt writer</a> is optional; save it for
    your own project ideas.
  </p>
</div>
